module

public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Modules.QuotientRingSheafEpi

/-!
# Recognising a quotient of sheaves of modules from the subobject it is a quotient by

Let `f₁ : M₁ ⟶ N` and `f₂ : M₂ ⟶ N` be two morphisms of sheaves of modules with a common
target. Their cokernels are the quotients of `N` by the subobjects the two morphisms generate,
and those subobjects are the *local closures* of the objectwise images
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.localClosure` of `Stacks.SheafOfModules.presheafRangeSubmodule`): a
section of `N` lies in the subobject generated by `f` when it is in the image of `f` after
restriction along the arrows of some covering sieve, not when it is in that image on the nose.

**This file is the resulting recognition.** If the two local closures have the same sections over
every object of the site, then the two cokernels are isomorphic, by an isomorphism carrying one
quotient map to the other. It is the counterpart for sheaves of *modules* of
`Stacks.Sites.quotientRingSheafIso`, which recognises a quotient of a sheaf of commutative rings
by a sheaf of ideals from its kernel on sections; the module-level statement is cleaner, because
nothing has to be said about the two sources `M₁` and `M₂`, only about the subobject of `N` they
cut out.

The point of stating it with local closures is that the hypothesis is *local* membership and not
objectwise membership. Two morphisms into `N` whose objectwise images agree have equal local
closures, so the objectwise comparison is a special case; but the submodules and sheaves of
ideals that arise in practice are local closures from the start — the ideal generated by a family
of sections, for instance — and their objectwise images need not agree at all.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelIsoCokernelLocalClosureRange`: the cokernel of a morphism of
  sheaves of modules is the cokernel of the inclusion of the local closure of its objectwise
  image, which is the image factorisation of the morphism read on cokernels.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq`: **the recognition.** Two morphisms
  into a common target whose objectwise images have the same local closure on sections have
  isomorphic cokernels.
* `Stacks.Sites.cokernelIsoOfSectionIdealEq`: the recognition at the free rank-one sheaf of
  modules over a sheaf of commutative rings, with its hypothesis read as an equality of the two
  ideals of sections. This is the form in which two quotients of one structure sheaf are
  compared.

## Main statements

* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoCokernelLocalClosureRange_hom`: the image
  factorisation read on cokernels carries the quotient map to the quotient map.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.localClosure_ι_comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero`: the subobject a morphism
  generates dies in the cokernel of that morphism.
* `Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq_hom`: the recognition
  carries one quotient map to the other, so it identifies *the quotient maps* and not merely the
  two objects. Without this an isomorphism of the two quotients says nothing about how either is
  presented.

## Implementation notes

**Why the subobjects are literally equal.** A submodule of a sheaf of modules is a family of
submodules of the sections together with two properties — stability under restriction, and
locality of membership — so two of them with the same sections over every object are equal, the
properties being propositions. `Mathlib.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.Submodule`'s `ext`
lemmas say exactly this. The recognition is therefore a comparison of two *vanishing* statements
and not of two universal properties: no local surjectivity or local injectivity argument is
needed, unlike at the ring level in `Stacks.Sites.isIso_quotientRingSheafDesc`.

**The two vanishing statements.** A morphism factors as an epimorphism onto the local closure of
its objectwise image followed by the inclusion of that subobject
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.toLocalClosureRange_ι`,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.epi_toLocalClosureRange`), so the inclusion dies in the cokernel of the
morphism; the subobjects being equal, the inclusion for `f₁` dies in the cokernel of `f₂` and
conversely, hence so do `f₁` and `f₂`. The two maps are then the ones the universal property of
the two cokernels produces, and they are mutually inverse because the quotient maps are
epimorphisms — the shape of
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.RegularIdealSheaf.cokernelSectionsHomIso`, which carries out this
argument for one generating family of one sheaf of ideals.

**Not stated through a transport.** Writing the recognition as the image factorisation on each
side with a transport along the equality of subobjects in between also proves it, but the
resulting term, with the equality of subobjects and the epimorphism-cancellation isomorphisms
inside it, exhausts the kernel's budget on the compatibility with the quotient maps. The two
`cokernel.desc`s cost nothing and make that compatibility `CategoryTheory.Limits.cokernel.π_desc`.
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite

namespace Stacks.SheafOfModules

universe v u u₁ v₁

variable {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C} {R : Sheaf J RingCat.{u}}
  [HasCokernels (_root_.SheafOfModules.{v} R)] {M₁ M₂ N : _root_.SheafOfModules.{v} R}

/-- **The cokernel of a morphism of sheaves of modules is the cokernel of the inclusion of the
subobject it generates**, namely of the local closure of its objectwise image. The morphism
factors through that subobject by a locally surjective, hence epimorphic, morphism
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.epi_toLocalClosureRange`), and a cokernel is unchanged by precomposition
with an epimorphism. -/
def cokernelIsoCokernelLocalClosureRange (f : M₁ ⟶ N) :
    cokernel f ≅ cokernel (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f.val)).ι :=
  haveI : Epi (toLocalClosureRange f) := epi_toLocalClosureRange f
  cokernelIsoOfEq (toLocalClosureRange_ι f).symm ≪≫
    cokernelEpiComp (toLocalClosureRange f) (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f.val)).ι

/-- The image factorisation read on cokernels carries the projection onto the cokernel of the
morphism to the projection onto the quotient by the subobject it generates. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoCokernelLocalClosureRange_hom (f : M₁ ⟶ N) :
    cokernel.π f ≫ (cokernelIsoCokernelLocalClosureRange f).hom =
      cokernel.π (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f.val)).ι := by
  have : Epi (toLocalClosureRange f) := epi_toLocalClosureRange f
  rw [cokernelIsoCokernelLocalClosureRange, Iso.trans_hom, ← Category.assoc,
    π_comp_cokernelIsoOfEq_hom]
  exact cokernel.π_desc _ _ _

/-- **The subobject a morphism of sheaves of modules generates dies in its cokernel.** The
morphism is an epimorphism onto that subobject followed by its inclusion, so the inclusion may
be cancelled against the vanishing of the morphism in its own cokernel. -/
theorem localClosure_ι_comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero (f : M₁ ⟶ N) :
    (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f.val)).ι ≫ cokernel.π f = 0 := by
  have : Epi (toLocalClosureRange f) := epi_toLocalClosureRange f
  rw [← cancel_epi (toLocalClosureRange f), comp_zero, ← Category.assoc,
    toLocalClosureRange_ι, cokernel.condition]

omit [HasCokernels (_root_.SheafOfModules.{v} R)] in
/-- **Two morphisms into a common target whose objectwise images have the same local closure on
sections generate the same subobject.** A submodule of a sheaf of modules is determined by its
sections, the stability and locality conditions being propositions. -/
theorem localClosure_presheafRangeSubmodule_eq (f₁ : M₁ ⟶ N) (f₂ : M₂ ⟶ N)
    (h : ∀ (X : Cᵒᵖ) (s : N.val.obj X),
      s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₁.val)).obj X ↔
        s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₂.val)).obj X) :
    localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₁.val) =
      localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₂.val) :=
  _root_.SheafOfModules.Submodule.ext
    (PresheafOfModules.Submodule.ext fun X => SetLike.ext (h X))

/-- **The first vanishing statement**: a morphism whose objectwise image has the same local
closure as that of a second morphism dies in the cokernel of the second. The subobjects the two
generate are equal, and the subobject a morphism generates dies in its cokernel. -/
theorem comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero_of_localClosure_eq (f₁ : M₁ ⟶ N) (f₂ : M₂ ⟶ N)
    (h : ∀ (X : Cᵒᵖ) (s : N.val.obj X),
      s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₁.val)).obj X ↔
        s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₂.val)).obj X) :
    f₁ ≫ cokernel.π f₂ = 0 := by
  have := localClosure_ι_comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero f₂
  rw [← localClosure_presheafRangeSubmodule_eq f₁ f₂ h] at this
  rw [← toLocalClosureRange_ι f₁, Category.assoc, this, comp_zero]

/-- **The recognition of a quotient of sheaves of modules.** Two morphisms into a common target
whose objectwise images have the same local closure on sections have isomorphic cokernels.

This is the module-level counterpart of `Stacks.Sites.quotientRingSheafIso`. The two maps are
those the universal property of the two cokernels produces from
`comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero_of_localClosure_eq`, and they are mutually inverse because the quotient
maps are epimorphisms. -/
def cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq (f₁ : M₁ ⟶ N) (f₂ : M₂ ⟶ N)
    (h : ∀ (X : Cᵒᵖ) (s : N.val.obj X),
      s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₁.val)).obj X ↔
        s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₂.val)).obj X) :
    cokernel f₁ ≅ cokernel f₂ where
  hom := cokernel.desc f₁ (cokernel.π f₂) (comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero_of_localClosure_eq f₁ f₂ h)
  inv := cokernel.desc f₂ (cokernel.π f₁)
    (comp_cokernelπ_eq_zero_of_localClosure_eq f₂ f₁ fun X s => (h X s).symm)
  hom_inv_id := by rw [← cancel_epi (cokernel.π f₁)]; simp
  inv_hom_id := by rw [← cancel_epi (cokernel.π f₂)]; simp

/-- **The recognition carries one quotient map to the other**, so it identifies the two
presentations of the quotient and not merely the two objects. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq_hom (f₁ : M₁ ⟶ N) (f₂ : M₂ ⟶ N)
    (h : ∀ (X : Cᵒᵖ) (s : N.val.obj X),
      s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₁.val)).obj X ↔
        s ∈ (localClosure N (presheafRangeSubmodule f₂.val)).obj X) :
    cokernel.π f₁ ≫ (cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq f₁ f₂ h).hom = cokernel.π f₂ :=
  cokernel.π_desc _ _ _

end Stacks.SheafOfModules

namespace Stacks.Sites


universe u

variable {C : Type u} [Category.{u} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C}
  [J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})] {O : Sheaf J CommRingCat.{u}}
  [HasSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{u}] [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]

/-- **The recognition of a quotient of the structure sheaf, read on the ideals of sections.**
Two morphisms into the free rank-one sheaf of `O`-modules which generate sheaves of ideals with
the same sections over every object of the site have isomorphic cokernels, by an isomorphism
carrying one quotient map to the other
(`Stacks.Sites.cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoOfSectionIdealEq_hom`).

This is `Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq` with its hypothesis rewritten:
`Stacks.Sites.idealOfHom` is by definition the local closure of the objectwise image, and the
ideal of sections of a sheaf of ideals is its value on sections, so the two hypotheses are the
same statement. It is the form a comparison of two quotients of one structure sheaf takes,
`Stacks.Sites.sectionIdeal` being how such comparisons are recorded. -/
def cokernelIsoOfSectionIdealEq
    {F₁ F₂ : SheafOfModules.{u} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)}
    (φ₁ : F₁ ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O))
    (φ₂ : F₂ ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O))
    (h : ∀ X : Cᵒᵖ, sectionIdeal (idealOfHom φ₁) X = sectionIdeal (idealOfHom φ₂) X) :
    cokernel φ₁ ≅ cokernel φ₂ :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq φ₁ φ₂
    fun X s => SetLike.ext_iff.1 (h X) s

omit [J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ CommRingCat.{u} RingCat.{u})] in
/-- The recognition on ideals of sections carries one quotient map to the other. -/
@[reassoc (attr := simp)]
theorem cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoOfSectionIdealEq_hom
    {F₁ F₂ : SheafOfModules.{u} (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O)}
    (φ₁ : F₁ ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O))
    (φ₂ : F₂ ⟶ SheafOfModules.unit (Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf O))
    (h : ∀ X : Cᵒᵖ, sectionIdeal (idealOfHom φ₁) X = sectionIdeal (idealOfHom φ₂) X) :
    cokernel.π φ₁ ≫ (cokernelIsoOfSectionIdealEq φ₁ φ₂ h).hom = cokernel.π φ₂ :=
  Stacks.SheafOfModules.cokernelπ_comp_cokernelIsoOfLocalClosureRangeEq_hom φ₁ φ₂ _

end Stacks.Sites

end
